{"name":"Yves Klein Blue","hex":"#00008b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yves Klein Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Yves Klein Blue was an Australian indie rock band from Brisbane, which formed in 2005 and disbanded in 2010. The line-up included Chris Banham on drums and percussion, Sean Cook on bass guitar, Charles Sale on lead guitar and keyboards and Michael Tomlinson on lead vocals and rhythm guitar. Their name is from the colour, International Klein Blue, by Yves Klein."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Yves_Klein_Blue_1.jpg/330px-Yves_Klein_Blue_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yves_Klein_Blue","wikidata":"Q8062325"},{"title":"International Klein Blue","blocks":[{"text":"International Klein Blue (IKB) is a deep blue hue first mixed by the French artist Yves Klein. IKB's visual impact comes from its heavy reliance on ultramarine, as well as Klein's often thick and textured application of paint to canvas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/IKB_191.jpg/330px-IKB_191.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Klein_Blue","wikidata":"Q534583"},{"title":"Yves Klein","blocks":[{"text":"Yves Klein was a French artist and an important figure in postwar European art. He was a leading member of the French artistic movement of Nouveau réalisme, founded in 1960 by art critic Pierre Restany. Klein was a pioneer in the development of performance art, and is seen as an inspiration to and as a forerunner of minimal art, as well as pop art. He developed and used International Klein Blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Yves_Klein_in_front_of_one_of_his_canvases_%281962%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Yves_Klein_in_front_of_one_of_his_canvases_%281962%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yves_Klein","wikidata":"Q154335"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ultramarinepigment.jpg/500px-Ultramarinepigment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ultramarinepigment.jpg/500px-Ultramarinepigment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Synthetic ultramarine, similar to that used in IKB pigment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUltramarinepigment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/63/Le_Vide.jpg/330px-Le_Vide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/63/Le_Vide.jpg/330px-Le_Vide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Le Vide (The Void) displayed at the Iris Clert Gallery","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALe_Vide.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/YvesKlein.Schwammrelief.JPG/500px-YvesKlein.Schwammrelief.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/YvesKlein.Schwammrelief.JPG/500px-YvesKlein.Schwammrelief.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sponge Relief (1959), Musiktheater im Revier, Gelsenkirchen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYvesKlein.Schwammrelief.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6e/Yves_Klein%2C_Le_Saut_Dans_le_Vide%2C_1960.jpg/250px-Yves_Klein%2C_Le_Saut_Dans_le_Vide%2C_1960.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6e/Yves_Klein%2C_Le_Saut_Dans_le_Vide%2C_1960.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6e/Yves_Klein%2C_Le_Saut_Dans_le_Vide%2C_1960.jpg/250px-Yves_Klein%2C_Le_Saut_Dans_le_Vide%2C_1960.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Le Saut dans le vide (Leap into the Void); photomontage by Shunk-Kender (Harry Shunk and Janos Kender) of a performance by Klein at Rue Gentil-Bernard, Fontenay-aux-Roses, October 1960","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYves_Klein%2C_Le_Saut_Dans_le_Vide%2C_1960.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common